# Why this is a BST model

A BST on a fixed ordered universe $k_0<\cdots<k_{n-1}$ has no freedom in
its labels once its shape is known: inorder must read precisely
$k_0,\ldots,k_{n-1}$. Conversely, assigning these keys to the nodes of any
$n$-node shape in inorder gives a valid BST. Forgetting the labels and
restoring them are inverse operations. We therefore use ranks $0,\ldots,n-1$
as keys, and store only shapes. This removes an invariant, not any possible
BST shape or access sequence.

A rotation preserves inorder order. Thus erasing labels before a rotation
and restoring them afterward gives exactly the usual labelled rotation.
If the final tree is \code{node l r}, its root's key is $\sz{l}$.
Requiring $\sz{l}=x$ therefore says exactly that key $x$ has been brought
to the root. This representation correspondence is the first result to
formalize in Lean.

\textbf{Why require the requested key at the root?}
A pointer search reaching depth $d$ can expose its key at the root by $d$
rotations. Reversing those rotations restores the searched tree, if needed.
The restoration can begin the next access phase, so our phase boundary can
always be placed at the instant the request is at the root. This explains
why root exposure need not force the algorithm to retain a particular
restructuring strategy.

\textbf{Which cost is equivalent?}
Derryberry's \href{https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jonderry/thesis.pdf}{\emph{Adaptive Binary Search Trees}}
(Sections~2.2 and~3.4, printed pages~12--13 and~25) describes a Wilber-style
root-access rotation model with cost $m+R$: $m$ requests and $R$ rotations.
Its comparison with the connected node-touch model uses lazy handling of
rotations outside the active connected region. The geometric BST model is
also discussed in \href{https://people.csail.mit.edu/mip/papers/arboral/paper.pdf}{Demaine et al.,
\emph{The Geometry of Binary Search Trees}}, Section~2.1.
These give the literature setting for the intended constant-factor simulation;
they are not Lean proofs about the definitions below.

Our file deliberately records just $R$. For standard total-cost comparisons,
use
\[
  \cost_{\mathrm{total}}(t,X)=\sz{X}+\cost_{\mathrm{rot}}(t,X).
\]
The extra request charge is essential: repeatedly requesting the current
root costs zero rotations, but positive standard search cost.
In particular, the current \code{DynamicallyOptimal} predicate concerns
rotation cost; its converse connection to conventional dynamic optimality
cannot be obtained by simply dropping the $m$ term.

\textbf{What remains to formalize.}
Besides the label/shape correspondence, we need costed simulations between
root-access rotations and a chosen pointer model. Rotations deep in a subtree
cannot simply be declared to have constant pointer cost: locating them must
be justified by a global simulation. For online algorithms, the translation
must also preserve the lack of future information. Exact final-tree agreement
may require delayed work; the interface and initial-tree allowance must be
stated explicitly in the simulation theorem.

\newpage
The rest of the document explains every definition in \code{RotationBST.lean}.
Each explanation is followed by the corresponding Lean code, extracted from
that file. The source remains authoritative. It imports only \code{Std};
all declarations live in the namespace \code{RotationBST}.

# Trees and implicit keys

A ***tree*** is either empty, written \code{nil}, or consists of a node with
a left and a right subtree, written \code{node l r}. Only nonempty nodes
represent keys. Empty subtrees have no key.

\leansnippet{Tree}

The declaration \code{inductive} gives exactly these two ways to construct a
finite tree. The \code{deriving} clause supplies decidable equality and printable
representations; it adds no mathematical condition.
The ***size*** $\sz{t}$ counts the keys in $t$:

\leansnippet{Tree.size}

Inorder visits the left subtree, the root, then the right subtree.
Thus the root's implicit key is $\sz{l}$. More generally, a subtree whose keys
start at $b$ has root key $b+\sz{l}$. The offset matters: a node's key is its
rank in the *whole* tree, not just in its own subtree.

: figure[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.87]
    \node[key] (r) at (0,0) {3};
    \node[key] (a) at (-2,-0.85) {1}; \node[key] (b) at (2,-0.85) {5};
    \node[key] (a0) at (-3,-1.7) {0}; \node[key] (a2) at (-1,-1.7) {2};
    \node[key] (b4) at (1,-1.7) {4}; \node[key] (b6) at (3,-1.7) {6};
    \foreach \u/\v in {r/a,r/b,a/a0,a/a2,b/b4,b/b6} \draw[edge] (\u)--(\v);
    \node[font=\small,anchor=west,text=black!65] at (3.7,-0.8)
      {\shortstack[l]{Inorder: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6$\\Root key: $\sz{l}=3$}};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption: Labels are shown only to explain the shape; the Lean value stores none
    of them. Missing children denote \code{nil}.
  @=fig:labels

\newpage
# One rotation

A ***rotation*** changes the shape locally while preserving inorder order.
The first two constructors are the familiar right and left rotations.

\leansnippet{Rotate}

The type \code{Tree → Tree → Prop} means that \code{Rotate s t} is a
proposition relating a source tree $s$ to a destination tree $t$.
Each constructor is a rule for proving such a proposition.
The variables $a,b,c$ stand for arbitrary subtrees, including empty ones.

: figure[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \node[key] (x) at (0,0) {$x$}; \node[target] (y) at (-0.9,-0.85) {$y$};
    \node[subtree] (a) at (-1.55,-1.9) {$A$};
    \node[subtree] (b) at (-0.25,-1.9) {$B$};
    \node[subtree] (c) at (1,-1.1) {$C$};
    \foreach \u/\v in {x/y,y/a,y/b,x/c} \draw[edge] (\u)--(\v);
    \node[target] (Y) at (6,0) {$y$}; \node[key] (X) at (6.9,-0.85) {$x$};
    \node[subtree] (A) at (5,-1.1) {$A$};
    \node[subtree] (B) at (6.25,-1.9) {$B$};
    \node[subtree] (C) at (7.55,-1.9) {$C$};
    \foreach \u/\v in {Y/A,Y/X,X/B,X/C} \draw[edge] (\u)--(\v);
    \draw[->,thick,figblue] (1.7,-0.6)--node[above,font=\small]{right rotation}(4.2,-0.6);
    \draw[<-,thick,figblue] (1.7,-1.05)--node[below,font=\small]{left rotation}(4.2,-1.05);
    \node[font=\small] at (3,-2.75) {Same inorder: $A,\ y,\ B,\ x,\ C$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption: The highlighted node is promoted by a right rotation. Its implicit
    key remains $\sz{A}$; the other displayed node keeps key $\sz{A}+1+\sz{B}$.
  @=fig:rotation

The constructors \code{inLeft} and \code{inRight} allow the same rotation
inside either child. Repeated applications locate a rotation at any depth.
They do not add rotations to the count: each proof of \code{Rotate s t}
represents one local rotation, wherever it occurs.

# A sequence of rotations

We write \code{Rotations s t k} when exactly $k$ rotations transform $s$ into $t$.

\leansnippet{Rotations}

The rule \code{refl} is the empty sequence. The rule \code{step} performs one
rotation from $s$ to $t$, followed by $k$ rotations from $t$ to $u$, giving $k+1$
rotations in total. The intermediate tree connects consecutive operations.
Implicit variables such as $s,t,u,k$ are inferred from the rule's use.

\newpage
# An access

An ***access to key $x$*** starts at $s$, performs $k$ rotations, and finishes
at a nonempty tree $t$ whose root has key $x$.
Because keys are inorder ranks, this last condition says that the left
subtree of $t$ has size $x$.

\leansnippet{Access}

The conjunction \code{∧} requires both a legal rotation sequence and the
right final root. The \code{nil} case is false because an empty tree cannot
serve any access. We do not separately require $x<\sz{s}$: that follows
from the definition and size preservation.

: figure[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
    \node[key] (a0) at (0,0) {0}; \node[key] (a1) at (0.65,-0.7) {1};
    \node[target] (a2) at (1.3,-1.4) {2};
    \draw[edge] (a0)--(a1)--(a2);
    \node[key] (b1) at (4,0) {1}; \node[key] (b0) at (3.3,-0.8) {0};
    \node[target] (b2) at (4.7,-0.8) {2};
    \draw[edge] (b1)--(b0); \draw[edge] (b1)--(b2);
    \node[target] (c2) at (8,0) {2}; \node[key] (c1) at (7.35,-0.7) {1};
    \node[key] (c0) at (6.7,-1.4) {0};
    \draw[edge] (c2)--(c1)--(c0);
    \draw[->,thick,figblue] (1.65,-0.4)--node[above,font=\small]{1 rotation}(2.8,-0.4);
    \draw[->,thick,figblue] (5.1,-0.4)--node[above,font=\small]{1 rotation}(6.5,-0.4);
    \node[font=\small] at (4,-2) {$\code{Access}\ s\ t\ 2\ 2$: the final left subtree has two nodes.};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption: Accessing key $2$ by two left rotations. The labels stay attached
    to their inorder positions throughout.
  @=fig:access

# A request sequence

An ***execution*** serves a finite list of requests in order. Its parameters
record the starting tree, request list, final tree, and total rotation count.

\leansnippet{Execution}

The empty list costs zero and leaves the tree unchanged. For \code{x :: xs},
first serve $x$, then serve the remaining requests from the resulting tree;
the costs add. No online restriction is imposed on how these witnesses are
chosen, so executions also describe the offline comparator.

The file proves three basic facts (only their statements are shown here):

\leansnippet{Invariants}

Rotations preserve size, hence preserve the key universe. Every successful
access requests an existing key. The first two facts follow by induction on
the corresponding constructors; the third follows from the final root condition.

\newpage
# An online algorithm

An ***algorithm*** chooses a new tree and a rotation count for each request.
It receives the past requests, the current tree, and the current request.
It must certify a legal access whenever the requested key exists.

\leansnippet{Algorithm}

The field \code{step} returns a pair $(u,k)$. Lean's projections \code{.1}
and \code{.2} select its tree and count. The field \code{correct} proves that
these choices satisfy \code{Access} for every valid request.
Invalid requests have no correctness requirement.

The interface gives the rule no future requests. Its persistent information
is represented by the current tree and the supplied request history; there
is no additional private-state field. The rotation sequence itself is
certified to exist by \code{Access}, rather than returned as a list of commands.

# Running the algorithm

The function \code{runFrom} applies the rule to successive requests,
carrying the history and current tree forward and adding the costs.

\leansnippet{Algorithm.runFrom}

The expression \code{past ++ [x]} appends $x$ to the history.
Although \code{runFrom} receives the remaining list \code{xs},
\code{step} receives only \code{past}, \code{t}, and \code{x}.
The recursion terminates because each call processes a shorter request list.

The ***cost*** starts with an empty history and selects the total count:

\leansnippet{Algorithm.cost}

For valid request sequences, successive applications of \code{correct}
give an \code{Execution} with precisely this final tree and total cost.
The online choices can therefore be compared directly with offline executions.

This is a mathematical cost model: computation used to choose rotations,
inspect shapes, or update the Lean lists is uncharged. There is no claim
here about the running time of the Lean evaluator or a machine implementation.

\newpage
# Dynamic optimality

An online algorithm is ***dynamically optimal in this model*** if a constant
factor times any offline execution's cost, plus a linear initial-tree allowance,
bounds its cost on the same input.
Writing $n=\sz{t}$, the inequality is
\[
  \cost_a(t,X)\le Ck+Dn.
\]

\leansnippet{DynamicallyOptimal}

The constants $C,D\in\N$ are chosen before the tree and requests, so they are
uniform in both $n$ and the input sequence. The comparator begins at the same
tree $t$ and may finish at any $u$.
Quantifying over every legal \code{Execution} is equivalent to comparing with
the minimum cost whenever a legal execution exists: a nonempty set of natural
costs has a least element. This avoids introducing a separate OPT function.

# The working-set measure

The ***working-set number*** of $x$ is $n$ on its first access; otherwise it
is one plus the number of distinct keys requested since its last access.
The argument \code{past} lists previous requests in chronological order.

\leansnippet{workingSetNumber}

Reversing \code{past} puts the most recent request first.
The function \code{takeWhile (· != x)} stops just before the previous $x$;
\code{eraseDups} removes repetitions, and \code{length} counts the distinct
intervening keys. For example, with $n=5$:
\[
 w([],2)=5,\qquad w([2],2)=1,\qquad w([2,0,1,0],2)=3.
\]
In the last example, only keys $0$ and $1$ have intervened.

The sum charges each request $1+\lfloor\log_2 w\rfloor$, using \code{Nat.log2}:

\leansnippet{workingSetSum}

The history is updated after each request, exactly as for the online algorithm.
The additive one keeps this charge positive even for immediate repetitions.

\newpage
# The working-set bound

An algorithm satisfies the ***working-set bound*** when its total rotation
cost is at most a constant times the working-set sum, plus an initial-tree allowance:
\[
 \cost_a(t,X)
 \le C\sum_{i=1}^{m}\bigl(1+\lfloor\log_2 w_i\rfloor\bigr)
       +Dn\bigl(1+\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor\bigr).
\]

\leansnippet{HasWorkingSetBound}

The premise \code{∀ x ∈ xs, x < t.size} restricts attention to existing keys.
Size preservation ensures that the same universe remains available at every
later access. Again, $C$ and $D$ are uniform constants.
This is a sequence bound: it permits one expensive access to be compensated
by cheaper accesses elsewhere. It asserts no worst-case per-access bound.

For a nonempty tree, the allowance is $O(n\log(n+1))$.
The empty tree admits only an empty valid request sequence, so its total cost
is zero. Lean defines \code{Nat.log2 0} as zero, making the formula total.

# What has been defined, and what remains to prove

The code defines the model and two desired properties of algorithms.
It proves size preservation and the range condition for accesses.
It does not yet define a particular algorithm, prove that every valid access
can be served, or prove dynamic optimality or a working-set theorem for any
algorithm. Those are subsequent mathematical developments.

Two conventions deserve to stay visible when using these definitions.
First, every access must finish with the requested key at the root, and
accessing the current root may cost zero. Second, every local rotation costs
one, even deep inside a subtree; there is no cost for locating it.
Thus all bounds here refer to this rotation-only, root-access model.
The opening discussion explains the standard total-cost normalization and
the simulation results that remain to be formalized.

The representation needs no \code{Valid} predicate: every finite binary-tree
shape already determines a unique BST on its inorder ranks.
The remaining obligations concern transformations, access endpoints, and costs.

